WEBVTT

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.480
The very centre of Africa.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:01:02.160
The centre of two million square miles of dense tropical rainforest.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:21.680
At first glance, it seems deserted and eerily still. Not an easy place to live.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:34.000
But in fact, there is a greater concentration of animals here than anywhere else in Africa.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:47.440
And in this world, they must grab every opportunity. Competition is intense and unrelenting.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:58.400
Even the forest itself fights its corner with spines and poisonous sap.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:20.400
Here, every living thing must fight for its space.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.600
Such beauty.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.320
But the flower is self-serving, enticing animals into an unwitting alliance.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:55.520
Stingless bees. They have to work hard. The forest flowers make them do so by rationing their nectar, forcing each bee to visit and so pollinate at least a thousand brooms each day.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:13.440
For the bees, it's worth the effort. They need the nectar to make honey, which they store in pots. It's so precious, they keep it hidden beneath the bark of a tree.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:37.440
But their secret is out.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:57.440
Nothing is safe in this forest.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:10.880
The chimpanzees love honey.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:40.800
She seems oblivious to danger. A fall from up here could be fatal. But she does need a bigger stick.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:57.040
Only a chimp has the ability to break into a stingless bee's nest as well hidden as this.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:14.240
Chimps are extremely intelligent, but none is born with the skill to use a tool.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:44.160
Youngsters like this one must learn by watching.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:06:04.080
She uses special tools, one after another, to get all the honey she can. And in a few minutes, she destroys what took the bees years to build.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:18.960
In the rainforest, nothing is safe.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:39.200
Here in the Congo, there can be as many as 500 trees crammed into every acre. In the battle for space, some will rise to over 60 meters high in just a few decades.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:49.120
In a valley like this, there could be close on 1,000 different species of tree.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:07:00.160
Up here, the crowns barely touch. Each tree seems to respect its neighbor's space.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:08.800
When they germinate down on the forest floor, young trees and plants are less restrained.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:17.440
But every new generation fights it out in battles we can see by accelerating time.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:47.200
They must get light if they are to survive, and they squeeze, crush, and even slash one another in order to reach it.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:08:17.120
Despite the thick canopy above, some sunlight does filter through and allows a few low-light specialists to bloom near the forest floor.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:40.080
Down here, there are animals, too, that seek out the sunlight.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:50.640
The forest's largest predator, a female rock python.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:09:03.040
Her body is five meters long, weighs 100 kilos, and has 4,000 muscles that she uses to crush the life out of her victims.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:14.640
But right now, her need is not for food, it's for warmth.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:26.400
She finds a rare patch where a shaft of sunlight strikes the ground, and she begins to bask.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:35.920
She's cold-blooded, so this is the only way she can raise the temperature of her body.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:42.560
But now, she's becoming very warm indeed, more than 10 degrees hotter than usual.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:51.040
At 40 degrees centigrade, she's in danger of killing herself.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:10:00.800
Just in time, she moves off.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:14.080
She disappears below ground.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:23.760
This is her nest, and it's full of giant eggs.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:34.400
It's critical for the eggs' development that they stay above 30 degrees.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:50.560
Here, in this special filming burrow, she gently wraps her superheated body around the eggs, passing onto them the warmth of the sun.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:55.280
She has done this every day for three months.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:00.400
The repeated heat stress on her body is so great, it could be lethal.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:29.360
And at the very least, it will take three years for her to recover from incubating this one clutch of eggs.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:45.920
Her parchment-shelled eggs carry the pressed imprint of her scales, an indication of the strength of her embrace.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:12:07.360
At last, her efforts are rewarded.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:22.800
But the babies can't stay here. They must leave their sanctuary and find food in the tangled world above.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:50.800
They're over 60 centimeters long, already big enough to be a threat to the smaller inhabitants of the forest.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:13:00.800
But they are themselves vulnerable, particularly to other snakes.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:23.760
But this one is their mother. Unusually for snakes, her maternal instincts continue for days after her eggs hatch.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:37.680
Even so, the forest is such a dangerous place that only one in a hundred of her youngsters is likely to reach adulthood.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:45.120
Just occasionally, the competition eases.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:51.920
A tree suddenly produces fruit.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:14:20.960
It's a magnet for the creatures of the canopy, and they, in turn, as they feed, create a bonanza for those on the ground below.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:27.680
A mob of red river hogs.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:33.840
They have traveled over two miles through the thick undergrowth to get this fall of fruit.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:41.680
But in the African forest, little comes for free.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:46.000
This feast is a bribe.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:15:07.120
The hogs will carry the seeds in their stomachs and deposit them far from the parent tree.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.320
Night falls.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:25.440
But one living community, which is neither animal nor plant, continues its never-ending work in the darkness.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:38.160
This ground is alive with fungi that digest all the litter discarded by the forest.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:50.320
Some rare fungi do so with enzymes that are luminous.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:59.920
The local people call them chimpanzee fire.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:23.600
Without the fungi, the dead vegetation would pile so high the living trees would be submerged.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:35.440
Dawn. And a new day reveals just how much control the jungle has over its own environment.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:39.360
The forests of the Congo are the lungs of Africa.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:50.880
As they use the sunlight to build their tissues, so they release oxygen and water vapor into the air.

00:16:50.880 --> 00:17:00.240
Each hectare of forest produces as vapor almost 190,000 liters of water a year.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:06.720
So much that it creates its own weather.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:12.960
Clouds blanket the forest, driving up the humidity and temperature.

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:18.560
A storm is brewing.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:40.160
The Congo might be the richest part of Africa, but it's also the most violent.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:56.080
Each year, as many as 100 million lightning bolts strike the forest.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:05.840
That's more than anywhere else in the world.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:17.200
And with the lightning comes the rain.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:40.480
Up to 95% of the rain that falls here is generated by the forest itself.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:50.000
With the deluge will come change to the animals and to the forest.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.760
It's certainly perfect weather for frogs.

00:18:55.760 --> 00:19:02.480
The big storm is the cue for the most important climb of this frog's life.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:09.120
It's a male in search of a mate.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:24.400
But if he is to find one, he has to get to the top.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:20:06.960
He needs to keep his wits about him, for the rain also brings out hunters.

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:18.080
Easy does it.

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:21.680
The top at last.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:34.480
But he's late to the party.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:38.240
The higher a male sits, the further his voice will carry.

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:49.920
So the top slots are worth fighting for.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:21:19.200
And

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:20.720
he's won.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:25.400
He has the top place.

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:38.120
So now it's time to sing.

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:49.920
And a white bellied female responds.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:59.760
They join together to mate.

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:09.960
The loser will have to wait for the next storm before he sings again.

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:16.440
She lays her eggs on a blade of a long leaf and he, using his back legs, folds it over

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:32.600
and glues its two edges together, shutting the eggs inside.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:38.440
This sealed nest is the safest place these leaf-folding frogs can find to protect their

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:52.840
precious brood.

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:57.400
Within days the eggs are developing.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:08.160
The timing is perfect.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:14.640
The rain washes away the glue and the tadpoles slip out of the leaves into the growing puddles

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:28.000
below.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:47.200
The rainy season reaches its peak and the ground has been transformed.

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:50.100
The forest is flooded.

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:57.320
It's a new world.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:24:03.800
Fish swim in from the swollen streams, exploiting this newly created space, snapping up the

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:11.240
drowning insects.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:19.640
This is a butterfly fish.

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:26.480
A Congo Bechir.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:46.040
The hunter becomes the hunted.

00:24:46.040 --> 00:25:03.280
The butterfly fish is too quick and leaps out of danger.

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:28.520
The floods gradually drain back into the established waterways that run through the forest like

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:33.200
blood vessels.

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:37.800
There is so much water flowing through the forest that even little rivulets can carve

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:56.120
out the soil around an ancient boulder.

00:25:56.120 --> 00:26:03.160
This is the home of one forest creature that has lived here in the Congo for 44 million

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:08.160
years.

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:17.640
Picathartes.

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:26.960
These birds mate for life and the male reaffronts the bond by displaying to the female.

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:31.480
They're building a mud nest on the underside of the boulder's overhang where it'll be

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:35.480
protected from the rain.

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:55.880
The female takes the lead.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:13.200
The male doesn't seem quite so skillful.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:19.000
Oh dear.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:27.480
Luckily she can put things right.

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:32.560
Now she's collecting the soft furnishings.

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:44.520
He's brought some too, but he still can't get it right.

00:27:44.520 --> 00:28:06.280
In the end, the female seems satisfied with the finish and just in time.

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:14.680
It might look as if he has been banished into the rain, but in fact they're a great team.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:26.960
They share the incubation 12 hours on, 12 hours off for the next three weeks.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:48.080
On due course, there are mouths to feed and now the male must prove his worth.

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:53.080
Worms are a good start and he's doing well.

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:59.360
But the chicks are insatiable.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:21.360
Fortunately, other things are on the menu.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:31.360
He might be a poor nest builder, but he is redeeming himself now.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:36.480
Domestic bliss.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:42.400
Rocky overhangs are the only place where Piccathartes will build their nest, so they owe their home

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:52.960
to the stream that revealed the flank of the giant boulder.

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:58.800
This stream, and count as others like it, merged to form the great rivers of Central

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:11.440
Africa.

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:18.880
More than 450 billion litres of rainwater, travelling down thousands of rivers, are heading

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:24.000
west.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.280
The waters pick up speed as the rivers spill over the edge of the central plateau and create

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:42.800
giant cascades of white water.

00:30:42.800 --> 00:31:11.360
The Congo forces its way through the wildest, most untouched forest in the whole of Africa.

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:35.920
The Congo forces its way through the wildest, most untouched forest in the whole of Africa.

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:49.360
The

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:55.800
Congo River system drains an area the size of India, carrying the waters westwards towards

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:58.880
the Atlantic.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:07.720
Before it reaches the coast, the rivers broaden, forcing back the forest.

00:32:07.720 --> 00:32:12.640
And here, for the first time, there is space.

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:18.960
Wide, flat and safe.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:24.400
These stretches of sand attract visitors from the coast.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:40.120
Skimmers, searching for somewhere safe to settle.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:46.000
The lower mandible of their beaks is greatly elongated.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:53.800
They slice it through the surface of the water at 10 metres a second.

00:32:53.800 --> 00:33:04.680
Often when it hits a tiny fish, it will snap shut.

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:14.400
But why come upriver to these open sand flats?

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:26.740
This is the answer.

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:30.520
But this nursery will not exist for long.

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:35.640
Four weeks from now, it will be under 10 metres of water.

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:43.920
If by then these chicks can't fly, they will drown.

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:49.160
The problem for young skimmers is that when they hatch, the lower part of their beaks

00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:52.200
is the same size as the upper.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:34:09.120
While they wait for it to grow, they do their best to learn the skimming technique.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:17.240
Skim.

00:34:47.240 --> 00:35:12.240
Open spaces may be safe, but they give no protection against the driving rain.

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:36.000
These storms are a warning that the skimmers must soon leave. The river is already rising.

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:52.560
This year the kicks get away in time.

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:24.240
It's not just water that has the power to clear away through the forest. There are animals that can do that too.

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:47.240
They have created a network of pathways that crisscross the lowland forest and run for thousands of miles in all directions.

00:36:54.240 --> 00:37:09.240
These pathmakers are surprisingly stealthy. But as night falls, there's a chance of catching a glimpse of them.

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:36.240
Forest elephants are very social creatures, but in dense jungle it's hard for them to find one another.

00:37:36.240 --> 00:37:45.240
These elephants are lucky.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:38:01.240
Here in the Congo, there is one special place where they can meet and mingle. A place that the elephants have created for themselves.

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:19.240
And this is it.

00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:41.240
Tsangabai, the legendary village of elephants.

00:38:41.240 --> 00:39:01.240
As well as being a place where they can enjoy one another's company, this great clearing satisfies another craving. For salts.

00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:30.240
The salts lie deep under the mud, so the elephants have got to mine for them, which they do with high pressure water jets from their trunks.

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:40.240
The precious salts and the chance to socialize bring in elephants from far and wide.

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:47.240
If an elephant is in the mood to mate, this is the place to be.

00:39:47.240 --> 00:40:10.240
This young bull is in a state of must, a kind of sexual fury.

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:38.240
He is so pumped up by hormones and the excitements of the crowd that he seems to have taken leave of his senses.

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:47.240
But will throwing his weight about impress the females?

00:40:47.240 --> 00:40:56.240
The cows only become fertile once every two years, so opportunities to encounter one at the right time are not common.

00:40:56.240 --> 00:41:03.240
This could well be the first chance this young male has had.

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:19.240
He's lucky, and there are no older bulls around to put him in his place.

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:44.240
Just for a moment, he is king of the bay.

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:56.240
But his rule doesn't last for long.

00:41:56.240 --> 00:42:14.240
Enter another lusty bull, and a much bigger one.

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:31.240
But the young bull is still charged up with testosterone. Bold or foolish, he's going into battle.

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:44.240
He will be the thorn in their clutches.

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:58.240
Regardless, the charging will multicore with the woman's strength.

00:42:58.240 --> 00:43:21.200
He never really had a chance.

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:44.240
Zhangabai is a huge clearing, but it's still just a speck in this vast expanse of green.

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:54.920
Elephants might fell trees and carve pathways, but nothing natural can hold back this forest forever.

00:43:54.920 --> 00:44:00.120
Nothing but the Atlantic Ocean.

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:28.280
Loango Beach on Africa's west coast, one of the last truly wild places where the concho jungle meets the sea.

00:44:28.280 --> 00:44:50.720
Here, the forest gives way to sand, surf and fresh sea air.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:59.040
The cool breezes and warm sunlight entice creatures out of the dark forest.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:20.640
The forest buffalo appear first.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:33.720
And here in the surf, they're a hippo.

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:52.840
Spray blows in from the sea, making the grass salty.

00:45:52.840 --> 00:46:00.960
So here, elephants can get their tasty salt without having to dig for it.

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:05.600
This mother with her tiny baby can feel the sun on her back.

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:09.680
Here, it's safe for her little one.

00:46:09.680 --> 00:46:14.520
They're free to eat in peace.

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:33.320
The bulls have all the room they need, so there's less risk of a fight.

00:46:33.320 --> 00:46:57.200
Everyone from gorillas to forest hogs ventures out to relax on the beach.

00:46:57.200 --> 00:47:24.280
But the forest creatures can't stay out here forever.

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:30.960
Despite everything, the intense competition, the threats, the darkness, they need their

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:56.240
forest just as their forest needs them all.

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:12.240
The Congo rainforest, a four-day journey to the heart of Africa.

00:48:12.240 --> 00:48:17.200
Once the plane leaves, you're on your own.

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:22.360
This expedition planned to film two of the Congo's best kept secrets, but to even find

00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:27.280
them, the crew would have to work very hard indeed.

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:35.520
You might as well be on a different planet, coming to a place like this, Planet Congo.

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:43.480
Mysterious, difficult, complex, challenging.

00:48:43.480 --> 00:48:53.480
You know, everything's trying to bite you, suck your blood.

00:48:53.480 --> 00:49:07.520
I've been tickled by a million feathers at the same time.

00:49:07.520 --> 00:49:14.320
The insects might be torture, but that's the least of their worries.

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:19.840
The only way to get deep into the jungle is to follow these trails.

00:49:19.840 --> 00:49:24.200
Tentacles made by dangerous forest elephants.

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:29.560
Well the first thing you need to know about forest elephants is you don't want to meet

00:49:29.560 --> 00:49:30.560
one.

00:49:30.560 --> 00:49:35.400
Because running away can elicit a charge, and it could be exactly the wrong thing to

00:49:35.400 --> 00:49:36.400
do.

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:51.560
Our team are completely dependent on their Bayaka guides for safety.

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:55.460
But it's these same forest elephants that James has come to film.

00:49:55.460 --> 00:50:02.600
And just to make the challenge harder, he's here to film them in the dark.

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:05.200
Nobody knows exactly what they get up to at night.

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:08.120
They haven't been filmed like this before.

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:14.840
I can rig this place if the Bayaka weren't here.

00:50:14.840 --> 00:50:17.160
Watching my back, really.

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:28.200
James needs to operate the remote cameras from somewhere out of the elephant's reach.

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:36.800
A tree platform seems like the best option.

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:39.280
Apparently they have very big elephants right now.

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:44.920
They want us to put it a bit higher, so I think I'll do what they say.

00:50:44.920 --> 00:50:49.560
But no one wants to stay out at night and help James with the filming.

00:50:49.560 --> 00:51:02.360
So James will be alone until morning.

00:51:02.360 --> 00:51:04.840
If anything goes wrong, he's on his own.

00:51:04.840 --> 00:51:07.200
If they really wanted to, they could push these trees over.

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:12.800
Can't imagine that's going to be an issue.

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:17.200
As James settled down for the night, he's got no idea of the trouble that's coming

00:51:17.200 --> 00:51:20.880
his way.

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:28.400
What's going on?

00:51:28.400 --> 00:51:32.840
Twenty miles away, Mark McEwan is also up in the middle of the night.

00:51:32.840 --> 00:51:37.240
The animal he's after is proving impossible to find.

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:45.480
So we get up in the darkness and we walk through the jungle at night, hoping to hear the sound

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:50.120
of cracking branches or leaves moving up in the trees.

00:51:50.120 --> 00:51:54.880
That means chimpanzees are stirring in the tree tops and Mark is here to film chimps

00:51:54.880 --> 00:51:58.640
hunting for honey.

00:51:58.640 --> 00:52:03.640
There's one chimp in particular he needs to find, a teenager with a very sweet tooth,

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:09.040
known to go further in the pursuit of a bee's nest than any other.

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:20.760
I've spent six days walking, probably the equivalent of half marathon every day in 100%

00:52:20.760 --> 00:52:25.520
humidity and about 95 degrees in the shade.

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:31.640
And we just can't find our chimpanzee.

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:39.400
Time is ticking away and Mark is running out of filming days.

00:52:39.400 --> 00:52:43.520
At the moment I just need some good luck.

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:46.120
We've come an awfully long way to get this sequence.

00:52:46.120 --> 00:52:53.240
We've got probably 10, 12 days left, but it's hard work at the moment.

00:52:53.240 --> 00:53:02.880
Walmart canoe is persevere and hope for a break.

00:53:02.880 --> 00:53:06.680
But not all the forest creatures are so shy.

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:11.120
Perched high in his tree, James Aldred is waiting patiently for the elephants to come

00:53:11.120 --> 00:53:12.120
in.

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:23.640
At last the elephants are here, but they're behaving strangely.

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:34.680
Now something's not quite right.

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:44.320
The elephants seem agitated.

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:47.560
They just want to get rid of you.

00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:52.680
One begins to thump the tree with its head.

00:53:52.680 --> 00:53:59.960
James has no option but to weather the attack.

00:53:59.960 --> 00:54:07.320
Just lean forward and keep headbutting, keep headbutting, keep headbutting.

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:18.000
Suddenly the cameras cut out and James is left in complete darkness.

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:23.200
After three weeks searching, Mark has lost nearly two stone in weight, but he hasn't

00:54:23.200 --> 00:54:24.360
given up.

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:30.840
He can't afford to put down his camera for a second.

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:33.480
Suddenly the guide spots the honey hunter.

00:54:33.480 --> 00:54:36.480
This is it.

00:54:36.480 --> 00:54:43.000
Where is she?

00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:54.600
She's inside the tree.

00:54:54.600 --> 00:55:01.880
She asks for some honey.

00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:02.880
She's risking her life.

00:55:02.880 --> 00:55:03.880
It's amazing.

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:27.560
I think I just couldn't stop smiling for several days after filming that.

00:55:27.560 --> 00:55:28.560
The relief is just unbelievable.

00:55:28.560 --> 00:55:45.720
Back at the camera platform, James has had a long night.

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:53.960
For over four hours, the elephant tried to shake him out of the tree.

00:55:53.960 --> 00:55:56.280
Got down this morning when the biarch came to collect me.

00:55:56.280 --> 00:56:00.760
Went to look at the camera and he pulled it out of the tree and he chewed through the

00:56:00.760 --> 00:56:01.760
power cable.

00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:04.440
He must have gotten a bit of a shock.

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:08.480
I mean only 12 volts, but it served him right quite honestly.

00:56:08.480 --> 00:56:15.280
But at least we got a shot of him before he trashed the camera.

00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:18.120
Silver lining.

00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:23.960
Despite this bumpy start, the elephant soon got used to James and James got used to the

00:56:23.960 --> 00:56:27.160
elephants.

00:56:27.160 --> 00:56:31.660
Filming here was never going to be easy, but we were soon able to reveal the night life

00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:52.860
of forest elephants like never before.

00:56:52.860 --> 00:57:02.960
Next time, Africa's Great Cape, where a land of mountains and deserts is flanked by two

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:08.440
coasts.

00:57:08.440 --> 00:57:15.240
Only in this part of Africa do powerful oceanic forces combine in such a riotous explosion

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:33.440
of life.
